{
  "comment": "Cross-implementation anchor for kernel/src/decision-log-transcribe.ts: how a decision record that the iterate ledgers ALREADY write maps onto the canonical ledger draft (5.8 R40 scenario 3, the 'three formats, one shape' convergence). A second implementation (the Python port in iterate-harness) is coded from this file, not from the TypeScript. Rules: (1) the record is an object with exactly the keys timestamp/round/type/data; an unknown key is REFUSED, never dropped. (2) type -> kind verbatim and must match ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,31}$; no case folding, no renaming. (3) round is copied and must be an integer >= 0; data -> payload in full, and the canonical-JSON length of payload must stay <= 8192 characters. (4) createdAt is timestamp normalized to ISO-8601 UTC milliseconds ('Z', 3 digits). The INPUT must match ^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}(\\.[0-9]{1,9})?(Z|\\+\\-[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2})$ -- an explicit zone is required, any offset converts to UTC, and fractional seconds TRUNCATE toward zero (drop the tail, never round up). Anything else is refused, including host-guessable spellings like 11/10/2026 02:28: one runtime reads those as US month/day and another refuses them, and a ledger whose dates depend on which runtime converted it is not evidence. (5) outcome is READ, never inferred from tone: data.outcome then data.status when one of pass|fail|blocked|inconclusive; else data.passed===true -> pass, data.passed===false -> fail; else inconclusive. (6) summary is the record's own sentence, first present and non-blank of data.summary, data.message, data.reason; when it carries none the label is `<kind> (round <round>)` (or just `<kind>` when round is absent) -- a label, not a claim; a sentence over SUMMARY_MAX_LENGTH=2048 is refused rather than truncated. (7) dimension is data.dimension when it matches ^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$ and is <= 64 chars, otherwise ABSENT -- never substituted by kind or method name. (8) operationId is data.operationId when it matches ^op_[0-9A-HJKMNP-TV-Z]{26}$, otherwise absent. (9) entryId is supplied by the caller (ledger-owned randomness), which is why this fixture pins a literal id per case: a transcription that minted ids could not be compared across implementations. (10) SIZE-CASE EXPANSION, so no 9 KB literals clutter this file: when a case carries `blobRepeat: N`, set record.data[ case.blobKey ?? 'blob' ] to N repetitions of 'x' BEFORE transcribing; when it carries `summaryRepeat: N`, set record.data.summary to N repetitions of 's'. Expectations otherwise compare field by field; an absent field in `expect` must be absent from the result, not null.",
